nodecode-print3d is a rewrite of mfranzon's mfranzon/print3d:

  https://github.com/mfranzon/print3d
  commit ff98a8c07253a90f5e4afefa42c2ffd893797eec
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 mfranzon (LICENSE)

The rewrite keeps the plugin's working loop without its Python server. What it
keeps: the job folder and its spec, where every dimension carries where it
came from (the user, a shop listing, or assumed with a reason); the cheap
printability checks on a triangle mesh before anything is sliced -- size
against the X2D bed, volume, manifold, whether the part sits on the bed, each
note with the fix to apply, which is what the plugin's dfm.py and
printability.py said in the model's own words; the model.py-is-the-model
convention, run headless through the user's own Blender and exported to a
path handed to the script after `--`; and the slice through the user's own
Bambu Studio with --ensure-on-bed, as the plugin's studio.py built it.

What is left out is everything that needed Python imaging and a browser: the
photo measuring (scale from a reference card, rectified faces, point picking
on a grid crop, tolerances per view), the likeness overlay against an
outlined photo, the HTML viewer with the model in three.js, the handoff print
sheet, the fit coupon, the pinned slicer profiles and the parts-and-fits
guide. The cell also does not read back the sliced .3mf's estimated time and
filament use, which the plugin parsed out of the plate gcode; the .3mf is
handed over as a file. The job spec is a Lisp-readable file rather than
JSON, because nothing but this cell reads it.
